{"name":"Chesty Bond","hex":"#516fa0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chesty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having large breasts; busty.","Not dry; involving the coughing of phlegm."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chesty"},{"term":"bond","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A document constituting evidence of a long-term debt, by which the bond issuer (the borrower) is obliged to pay interest when due, and repay the principal at maturity, as specified on the face of the bond certificate. The rights of the holder are specified in the bond indenture, which contains the legal terms and conditions under which the bond was issued. Bonds are available in two forms: registered bonds, and bearer bonds.","A documentary obligation to pay a sum or to perform a contract; a debenture."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To connect, secure or tie with a bond; to bind.","To cause to adhere (one material with another)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bond"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chesty Bond","blocks":[{"text":"Chesty Bond is a fictional cartoon character and trademark for the Australian clothing company Bonds. The character was created in 1940, a co-creation of the advertising account manager Ted Moloney and artist Syd Miller. Chesty Bond was conceived as a likeable and heroic character in a continuous newspaper comic-strip, intended as a marketing campaign to sell the Bonds Athletic singlet. The comic-strip format, with a constantly changing storyline, proved to be extremely popular and continued to be published until 1963. Chesty Bond was possibly the world's first daily advertising comic-strip. By virtue of its popularity and longevity, Chesty Bond became absorbed into Australian popular culture as a national icon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/b/bc/Chesty_Bond_logo.svg/330px-Chesty_Bond_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chesty_Bond","wikidata":"Q5094027"},{"title":"Chesty (mascot)","blocks":[{"text":"Chesty, a Bulldog, is the unofficial mascot of the United States Marine Corps. The first Chesty was named after Chesty Puller—the most highly decorated Marine in history—in July 1957. The current dog, Chesty XVII, is the 17th mascot named Chesty. Chesty's duties include attending drills, parades, and community events."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Teufel_Hunden_poster.jpg/330px-Teufel_Hunden_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chesty_(mascot)","wikidata":"Q131782878"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/First_Chesty_Bond_comic_strip_TheSun_19Mar1940.jpg/500px-First_Chesty_Bond_comic_strip_TheSun_19Mar1940.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/First_Chesty_Bond_comic_strip_TheSun_19Mar1940.jpg/1280px-First_Chesty_Bond_comic_strip_TheSun_19Mar1940.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/First_Chesty_Bond_comic_strip_TheSun_19Mar1940.jpg/500px-First_Chesty_Bond_comic_strip_TheSun_19Mar1940.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first 'Chesty Bond' comic-strip, illustrated by Syd Miller, published in The Sun (Sydney), 19 March 1940.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFirst_Chesty_Bond_comic_strip_TheSun_19Mar1940.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Chesty_Bond_confronts_Hitler_TheSun_11Jan1945.jpg/500px-Chesty_Bond_confronts_Hitler_TheSun_11Jan1945.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Chesty_Bond_confronts_Hitler_TheSun_11Jan1945.jpg/500px-Chesty_Bond_confronts_Hitler_TheSun_11Jan1945.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"'Chesty Bond' confronts Adolf Hitler (from Sydney's The Sun, 11 January 1945).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChesty_Bond_confronts_Hitler_TheSun_11Jan1945.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Meet_Chesty_Bond_BrisTele_28Oct1946.jpg/500px-Meet_Chesty_Bond_BrisTele_28Oct1946.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Meet_Chesty_Bond_BrisTele_28Oct1946.jpg/500px-Meet_Chesty_Bond_BrisTele_28Oct1946.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A panel from the introductory Chesty Bond comic-strip when it was first published in Brisbane's The Telegraph newspaper, 28 October 1946.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMeet_Chesty_Bond_BrisTele_28Oct1946.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jack_Lang_detail_Virgil_Reilly_SW_10Jun1933.jpg/250px-Jack_Lang_detail_Virgil_Reilly_SW_10Jun1933.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jack_Lang_detail_Virgil_Reilly_SW_10Jun1933.jpg/330px-Jack_Lang_detail_Virgil_Reilly_SW_10Jun1933.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jack_Lang_detail_Virgil_Reilly_SW_10Jun1933.jpg/250px-Jack_Lang_detail_Virgil_Reilly_SW_10Jun1933.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJack_Lang_detail_Virgil_Reilly_SW_10Jun1933.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Chesty_Bond_Syd_Miller_The_Sun_20Mar1940.jpg/250px-Chesty_Bond_Syd_Miller_The_S